<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chlothar I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 497–561</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Clotaire I, Chlothar the Old · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merovingian-dynasty">Merovingian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chlothar I was a Merovingian king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, the youngest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis I</a>. On his father's death in 511 he received a share of the kingdom around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soissons">Soissons</a>, and with his brothers he conquered the Burgundian kingdom in 534 and took part in the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>. Outliving his brothers and nephews, some of whom he murdered, he reunited the Frankish realm from 558, which was divided again among his sons in 561.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chlothar-i.jpg" alt="Chlothar I" width="320" height="149" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monnaie_d%27argent_de_Clotaire_Ier.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Chlothar was one of the four sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis I</a> who inherited shares of their father's kingdom in 511. His brothers were Theuderic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chlodomer">Chlodomer</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/childebert-i">Childebert I</a>. Chlothar received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soissons">Soissons</a>, which he made his capital, with Laon, Noyon, Cambrai, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maastricht">Maastricht</a> and the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a>, and a second part in Aquitaine. Merovingian custom divided an inheritance evenly among sons, so the kingdom was often split. Chlothar had five wives, more from political expediency than personal motive, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radegund">Radegund</a>, a Thuringian princess later canonised, and Ingund, by whom he had most of his sons. He was a less than enthusiastic Christian and introduced taxes on church property.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Chlothar spent most of his life expanding his territory at the expense of relatives and neighbours, while his brothers avoided open war with him by joining his attacks and sharing the spoils. In 524 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chlodomer">Chlodomer</a> was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-vezeronce">Battle of Vézeronce</a>. Chlothar and Childebert then took Chlodomer's kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>, and two of Chlodomer's young sons, Theodebald and Gunthar, were killed, according to the account given, by Chlothar himself. A third, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clodoald">Clodoald</a>, escaped and became a monk. With Theuderic he conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, and in 534, with Childebert and Theudebert, he overran the Burgundian kingdom, which was divided among the three.</p><p>When Theudebald died childless in 555, Chlothar took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>, and he put down a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> revolt. On 23 December 558 Childebert died without heirs, and Chlothar reunited the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Frankish kingdom</a>. His son Chram, who had set himself against him with Childebert's encouragement, was captured and put to death with his wife and daughters.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Chlothar died at the end of 561, at the age of 64, of acute pneumonia, and was buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soissons">Soissons</a>. His kingdom then covered all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimania">Septimania</a> and part of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. It was divided again among his four surviving sons: Charibert received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, Guntram Burgundy, Sigebert the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a> and Chilperic Soissons.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chlothar I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…family inheritance, dividing it among their sons, so that it was repeatedly split into kingdoms that came to be known as Austrasia in the east, Neustria in the west and Burgundy, and reunited when lines died out, as under <b>Chlothar I</b> in 558 and Chlothar II in 613.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-franks">Kingdom of the Franks · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>They were long a match for the Franks: in the 550s they inflicted a heavy defeat on <b>Chlothar I</b>, and in the 8th century they resisted repeated campaigns by Charles Martel and Pippin the Short.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saxons">Saxons · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Childebert died after a long illness without heirs, allowing his brother <b>Chlothar I</b> to reunite the Frankish kingdom, as their father Clovis I had done, and to seize Childebert's treasure.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-23">23 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>At the division of 561 <b>Chlothar I's</b> son Guntram received Burgundy with Orléans, ruling from Chalon-sur-Saône.</blockquote><a href="/state/frankish-kingdom-of-burgundy">Frankish Kingdom of Burgundy</a></li><li><blockquote>When Clovis died in 511, his youngest son <b>Chlothar I</b> received the old Frankish heartland in the north, between the Somme and the Scheldt, ruling from Soissons, the former seat of the Domain of Soissons.</blockquote><a href="/state/frankish-kingdom-of-soissons">Frankish Kingdom of Soissons</a></li><li><blockquote>His uncles Childebert I and <b>Chlothar I</b> murdered two of his young sons and divided his kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-orleans">Kingdom of Orléans</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soissons">Soissons</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soissons">Soissons</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.38,3.32&amp;year=538" aria-label="Soissons on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Chlothar I"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5185 5898h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5185 5898h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.38,3.32&amp;year=538">What was here in 538 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
